# Python Refactoring Standards

<rule>
name: python_refactoring
description: Standards for refactoring Python code safely and effectively
filters:
  - type: file_extension
    pattern: "\.py$"

actions:
  - type: suggest
    message: |
      ## Python Refactoring Guidelines

      ### Refactoring Principles
      1. Maintain behavior:
         - Ensure functionality remains unchanged
         - Preserve all existing interfaces
         - Keep backward compatibility when possible
         - Validate behavior with tests before and after

      2. Incremental approach:
         - Make small, focused changes
         - Commit frequently
         - Test after each change
         - Document refactoring steps

      ### Code Smell Detection
      1. Common Python code smells:
         - Long functions (>50 lines)
         - Deep nesting (>3 levels)
         - Large classes (>300 lines)
         - High cyclomatic complexity
         - Duplicate code
         - Global state
         - Excessive comments explaining complex code

      2. Detection tools:
         - Use static analyzers (pylint, flake8)
         - Monitor code metrics
         - Perform regular code reviews
         - Use @python-code-metrics tools

      ### Refactoring Techniques
      1. Function-level refactoring:
         - Extract method/function
         - Inline method/function
         - Rename method/function
         - Add/remove parameters
         - Replace algorithm

      2. Class-level refactoring:
         - Extract class
         - Move method
         - Pull up/push down field or method
         - Replace inheritance with composition
         - Extract interface/abstract class

      3. Module-level refactoring:
         - Move functionality between modules
         - Split module
         - Convert module to package
         - Reorganize imports

      ### Testing During Refactoring
      1. Test coverage:
         - Ensure high test coverage before refactoring
         - Add tests for uncovered code
         - Use both unit and integration tests
         - Follow @python-testing guidelines

      2. Test-driven refactoring:
         - Write tests for existing behavior
         - Refactor while keeping tests passing
         - Add tests for edge cases
         - Use test doubles when appropriate

      ### Refactoring Tools
      1. IDE refactoring tools:
         - Use automated rename/extract features
         - Leverage code analysis tools
         - Use preview features before applying changes
         - Verify changes after automated refactoring

      2. Version control:
         - Create dedicated refactoring branches
         - Use meaningful commit messages
         - Follow @python-git-workflow guidelines
         - Consider separate PRs for refactoring

      ### Related Guidelines
      - For testing practices, see @python-testing
      - For code metrics, see @python-code-metrics
      - For git workflow, see @python-git-workflow
      - For code organization, see @python-code-organization

